// Chronicle AP Euro: big-event profiles for the Event Test.
// [event title, what happened, key people, result, [true details], [plausible false details]]
// Units 2-4 follow the Khare + Harris Euro Notes; Unit 1 is standard AP Euro coverage (not from Dylan's notes).
const BIG = [
// ---- 1.1 Italian Renaissance ----
["Fall of Constantinople", "Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II besieges and captures Constantinople, ending the Byzantine Empire. Greek scholars and manuscripts flow west to Italy.", [], "The Ottomans become a major threat in southeastern Europe, and Italian humanists gain Greek texts.", ["The Ottomans used huge cannons against the city walls.", "It ended the thousand-year Byzantine (Eastern Roman) Empire."], ["Venetian crusaders captured the city.", "It immediately ended the Peace of Lodi."]],
["Peace of Lodi", "Milan, Venice, Florence, Naples, and the Papal States agree to stop fighting and keep a balance of power so no one state dominates Italy.", ["Cosimo de' Medici"], "About 40 years of relative peace in Italy, until the French invasion of 1494.", ["It set up a balance of power among Italy's five major states.", "It held until France invaded in 1494."], ["It united Italy under the pope.", "Charles V imposed it on the Italian states."]],
["Cosimo de' Medici takes control of Florence", "Cosimo de' Medici returns from exile and dominates the Florentine republic through wealth, patronage, and allies, usually without holding formal office.", ["Cosimo de' Medici"], "Florence keeps its republican forms while the Medici bank funds art and learning.", ["The Medici fortune came from banking.", "Cosimo ruled from behind the scenes rather than as a crowned prince."], ["Cosimo was crowned king of Florence.", "The Medici made their fortune in the Atlantic slave trade."]],
["French invasion of Italy", "Charles VIII of France invades Italy to claim the kingdom of Naples. The Medici are driven out of Florence and the friar Savonarola takes over.", ["Girolamo Savonarola"], "Italy becomes a battleground for France and Spain for decades, exposing how weak the divided city-states are.", ["Charles VIII claimed the kingdom of Naples.", "The Medici were expelled from Florence."], ["It was launched to stop the Protestant Reformation.", "The Italian states united and drove France out within a month."]],
["Machiavelli writes The Prince", "Exiled after the Medici return to Florence, Machiavelli writes a handbook arguing that rulers should do whatever works to gain and keep power.", ["Niccolò Machiavelli"], "A foundation of secular political thought: politics judged by results, not Christian ethics.", ["He argued it is safer to be feared than loved if a ruler can't be both.", "He wrote it in exile after the Medici returned to power."], ["He argued rulers are chosen by God and must obey the pope.", "He wrote it to defend Savonarola's government."]],
["Sack of Rome", "Unpaid, mutinous troops of Emperor Charles V plunder Rome for weeks while Pope Clement VII is trapped in his fortress.", ["Charles V"], "It ends the High Renaissance in Rome and leaves the pope under Charles V's influence, which matters when Henry VIII wants an annulment.", ["The troops were mutinous and unpaid.", "It left the pope dependent on Charles V."], ["Ottoman forces carried it out.", "Martin Luther ordered it."]],
// ---- 1.2 Printing and the Northern Renaissance ----
["Gutenberg Bible printed", "In Mainz, Johannes Gutenberg uses movable metal type to print a Latin Bible, proving books can be produced quickly and in large numbers.", ["Johannes Gutenberg"], "Printing spreads to hundreds of European towns, making books cheaper and ideas impossible to contain.", ["It used reusable metal type.", "It was printed in Mainz."], ["It was the first Bible translated into German.", "It was printed in Florence for the Medici."]],
["Erasmus's The Praise of Folly", "Erasmus writes a satire in which Folly mocks the vices of monks, theologians, and church officials, and calls for a simpler, sincere Christianity.", ["Desiderius Erasmus"], "It spreads Christian humanist criticism of the Church before Luther.", ["It is a satire narrated by Folly.", "It mocks corrupt clergy and empty rituals."], ["It called on Christians to leave the Catholic Church.", "It defended the sale of indulgences."]],
["Erasmus's Greek New Testament", "Erasmus publishes the New Testament in its original Greek with a fresh Latin translation, correcting errors in the Church's official text.", ["Desiderius Erasmus"], "Scholars like Luther use it; it shows the humanist idea of going back to the sources.", ["It was printed in Basel in 1516.", "It applied 'back to the sources' to Scripture."], ["It was the first English Bible.", "The pope commissioned it to defend the official Latin text."]],
["More's Utopia", "Thomas More describes an imaginary island with shared property and religious toleration to criticize the greed and injustice of Europe.", ["Thomas More"], "A model of humanist social criticism; More later dies opposing Henry VIII's break with Rome.", ["It describes an imaginary ideal society.", "It criticizes European greed and injustice."], ["It is a guide to absolute monarchy.", "It describes More's own travels in the Americas."]],
["Christine de Pizan's Book of the City of Ladies", "Christine de Pizan imagines building a city of virtuous women from history to answer male writers who attacked women's character and abilities.", ["Christine de Pizan"], "An early, famous defense of women's abilities and virtue.", ["It defends women against male writers' attacks.", "It uses examples of virtuous women from history."], ["It argued women should be kept out of education.", "Erasmus wrote it."]],
// ---- 1.3 New Monarchies ----
["Hundred Years' War ends", "France drives the English out of nearly all of France; only Calais remains English.", [], "The French crown emerges stronger, with a standing army and royal taxes.", ["England kept only Calais.", "It strengthened the French monarchy."], ["England won and kept Paris permanently.", "It ended with the Peace of Westphalia."]],
["Battle of Bosworth Field", "Henry Tudor defeats and kills Richard III, ending the Wars of the Roses, and becomes Henry VII.", ["Henry VII"], "The Tudor dynasty begins; Henry VII tames the nobles and rebuilds royal finances.", ["Richard III was killed in the battle.", "It founded the Tudor dynasty."], ["Henry VIII won it.", "It was fought between England and France."]],
["Ferdinand and Isabella marry", "Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castile marry, joining Spain's two largest kingdoms under shared rule, though each kingdom keeps its own laws.", ["Ferdinand of Aragon", "Isabella of Castile"], "Their joint rule completes the Reconquista, creates the Inquisition, and funds Columbus.", ["Each kingdom kept its own laws and institutions.", "It joined Aragon and Castile under shared rule."], ["It immediately created a single Spanish parliament.", "It joined Spain and Portugal."]],
["Spanish Inquisition established", "Ferdinand and Isabella set up a church court under royal control to root out converted Jews and Muslims suspected of secretly keeping their old faiths.", ["Ferdinand of Aragon", "Isabella of Castile"], "Religious uniformity becomes a tool of royal power in Spain.", ["The Spanish crown controlled it.", "It targeted converts suspected of secretly keeping their old faith."], ["It was founded to enforce the Council of Trent's decrees.", "It mainly targeted Protestants in England."]],
["Fall of Granada", "Ferdinand and Isabella conquer Granada, the last Muslim kingdom in Iberia, completing the centuries-long Reconquista.", ["Ferdinand of Aragon", "Isabella of Castile"], "Spain is united under Catholic monarchs, who soon expel the Jews and fund Columbus.", ["It completed the Reconquista.", "It happened in January 1492, before Columbus sailed."], ["Portugal conquered it.", "It happened after Columbus's first voyage."]],
["Concordat of Bologna", "The pope and King Francis I agree that the French king will appoint France's bishops and abbots.", ["Francis I"], "The French crown controls church offices and their wealth, one reason France had little reason to turn Protestant.", ["It let the French king appoint bishops and abbots.", "Francis I made it with the pope."], ["It made France Protestant.", "It let the pope tax French nobles directly."]],
// ---- 1.4 Protestant Reformation ----
["Luther's Ninety-Five Theses", "Martin Luther, a monk and professor at Wittenberg, challenges the sale of indulgences, provoked by Johann Tetzel's preaching.", ["Martin Luther", "Johann Tetzel"], "Printed copies spread across Germany within weeks, starting the Reformation.", ["It attacked the sale of indulgences.", "Luther taught at the University of Wittenberg."], ["It called for the execution of the pope.", "It answered the Council of Trent."]],
["Leipzig Debate", "Debating Johann Eck, Luther is pushed to say that popes and church councils can be wrong, and to defend some ideas of the condemned heretic Jan Hus.", ["Martin Luther", "Johann Eck"], "Luther's break with papal authority becomes open and hard to reverse.", ["Luther denied that popes and councils cannot err.", "Eck pushed Luther into defending ideas of Jan Hus."], ["Luther and Eck agreed to reunite the Church.", "It was a debate between Luther and Calvin."]],
["Diet of Worms", "Before Emperor Charles V, Luther refuses to recant his writings unless proven wrong by Scripture or plain reason, and he is declared an outlaw.", ["Martin Luther", "Charles V", "Frederick the Wise"], "Frederick the Wise hides him at Wartburg Castle, where he translates the New Testament into German.", ["Luther refused to recant.", "The Edict of Worms declared him an outlaw."], ["Luther recanted and was pardoned.", "Henry VIII presided over it."]],
["German Peasants' War", "Peasants rise up across Germany demanding an end to serfdom and heavy dues, citing Luther's ideas about Christian freedom. Luther urges the princes to crush them.", ["Martin Luther"], "The princes crush the revolt, and Lutheranism becomes tied to princely power and social order.", ["Peasants used Luther's ideas to justify their demands.", "Luther sided with the princes."], ["Luther led the peasants' army.", "The peasants won the abolition of serfdom."]],
["Marburg Colloquy", "Luther and Zwingli meet to unite their movements but split over the Eucharist: Luther insists Christ is really present, Zwingli says it is symbolic.", ["Martin Luther", "Ulrich Zwingli"], "Protestantism stays divided between Lutheran and Reformed branches.", ["They split over the Eucharist.", "It failed to unite Lutherans and Swiss reformers."], ["They split over indulgences.", "Calvin hosted it in Geneva."]],
["Anabaptists seize Münster", "Radical Anabaptists take over the city of Münster, share property, practice polygamy, and declare a kingdom before Catholic and Lutheran forces retake it.", [], "Anabaptists are persecuted by Catholics and Protestants alike as dangerous radicals.", ["Catholic and Lutheran forces together crushed it.", "Anabaptists rejected infant baptism."], ["Luther led it.", "It became a lasting Anabaptist state."]],
["Calvin settles in Geneva", "Calvin returns to Geneva and makes it a model Reformed city, with strict moral discipline enforced by church elders.", ["John Calvin"], "Geneva trains missionaries who spread Calvinism to France, Scotland, the Netherlands, and beyond.", ["Church elders policed moral behavior.", "Geneva trained missionaries who spread Calvinism."], ["Geneva became Lutheran.", "Calvin was permanently expelled that year."]],
// ---- 1.5 English Reformation ----
["Henry VIII seeks an annulment", "Henry VIII asks the pope to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, who has no surviving son. The pope, under pressure from Catherine's nephew Charles V, refuses.", ["Henry VIII", "Catherine of Aragon", "Charles V"], "Henry breaks with Rome to get his way.", ["Catherine of Aragon was Charles V's aunt.", "Henry wanted a male heir."], ["The pope granted it right away.", "Henry wanted to become a Lutheran."]],
["Act of Supremacy", "Parliament declares Henry VIII Supreme Head of the Church of England, cutting England off from papal authority.", ["Henry VIII", "Thomas Cromwell"], "The English church becomes a state church; refusing to accept it, like Thomas More, can mean death.", ["Parliament passed it.", "It made the king head of the English church."], ["It made England Calvinist in doctrine.", "Mary I passed it."]],
["Dissolution of the monasteries begins", "Thomas Cromwell closes England's monasteries, and the crown seizes their lands and sells them to nobles and gentry.", ["Thomas Cromwell", "Henry VIII"], "Landowners gain a financial stake in the break with Rome.", ["Monastic lands were sold to nobles and gentry.", "Thomas Cromwell organized it."], ["The lands were returned to the pope.", "Mary I carried it out."]],
["Mary I takes the throne", "Mary I, Catherine of Aragon's daughter, restores Catholicism, marries Philip II of Spain, and burns around 300 Protestants.", ["Mary I"], "Her persecution makes many English people hostile to Catholicism and to Spain.", ["She married Philip II of Spain.", "About 300 Protestants were burned."], ["She made England Protestant.", "She was Anne Boleyn's daughter."]],
["Elizabethan Settlement", "Elizabeth I's new Acts of Supremacy and Uniformity create a Protestant church that keeps bishops and some traditional ritual: a 'middle way.'", ["Elizabeth I"], "England stays Protestant; Puritans think it doesn't go far enough, and Catholics resist.", ["It kept bishops.", "Puritans thought it didn't go far enough."], ["It restored papal authority.", "It copied Geneva's Calvinist church exactly."]],
// ---- 1.6 Catholic Reformation ----
["Jesuits approved by the pope", "Pope Paul III approves Ignatius of Loyola's Society of Jesus, a disciplined order devoted to the pope.", ["Ignatius of Loyola", "Pope Paul III"], "Jesuits build schools, advise Catholic rulers, and send missionaries to Asia and the Americas.", ["They took a special vow of obedience to the pope.", "They were known for schools and missions."], ["Luther founded them.", "They lived as isolated hermits."]],
["Roman Inquisition established", "Paul III creates a central papal court to prosecute heresy, aimed at stopping Protestant ideas from spreading in Italy.", ["Pope Paul III"], "It later puts Galileo on trial (1633).", ["The papacy ran it.", "It later put Galileo on trial."], ["The Spanish crown controlled it.", "The Council of Trent abolished it."]],
["Council of Trent opens", "Meeting on and off from 1545 to 1563, the council reaffirms Catholic doctrine and reforms abuses in the Church.", ["Pope Paul III"], "Clear doctrine and better-trained clergy revive Catholicism, but no compromise with Protestants is possible.", ["It reaffirmed salvation through faith and works.", "It required seminaries to train priests."], ["It accepted salvation by faith alone.", "It allowed priests to marry."]],
["Index of Prohibited Books", "The papacy publishes a list of books Catholics are forbidden to read, including Protestant works.", [], "Censorship becomes a weapon against heresy in the age of printing.", ["It listed books Catholics could not read.", "It responded to printed Protestant ideas."], ["It listed books every Catholic had to own.", "Henry VIII created it."]],
// ---- 2.1 Exploration ----
["Columbus reaches the Americas", "Sailing west for Castile to reach Asia, Columbus lands in the Caribbean, believing he has reached the Indies.", ["Christopher Columbus", "Isabella of Castile"], "Spain begins the conquest and colonization of the Americas.", ["Castile sponsored him.", "He believed he had reached Asia."], ["Portugal sponsored him.", "He landed in Brazil."]],
["Treaty of Tordesillas", "Spain and Portugal draw a line through the Atlantic: newly found lands to the west go to Spain, to the east to Portugal.", [], "Portugal gets Brazil and the route around Africa; other powers ignore the deal.", ["It gave Portugal Brazil.", "It divided new lands between Spain and Portugal."], ["It gave England North America.", "It ended the Thirty Years' War."]],
["Vasco da Gama reaches India", "Da Gama sails around Africa and across the Indian Ocean to Calicut, opening a direct sea route from Europe to Asia.", ["Vasco da Gama"], "Portugal builds an Indian Ocean trading empire, bypassing Silk Road middlemen.", ["He sailed around Africa.", "He reached Calicut in India."], ["He sailed west across the Atlantic.", "He sailed for Spain."]],
["Cortés topples the Aztec Empire", "Hernán Cortés and a few hundred Spaniards, allied with Indigenous enemies of the Aztecs and helped by smallpox, conquer Tenochtitlan.", ["Hernán Cortés"], "Spain takes Mexico, which becomes New Spain.", ["Indigenous allies fought alongside the Spanish.", "Smallpox devastated the Aztecs."], ["Cortés commanded a Spanish army of 50,000.", "It was the conquest of the Inca."]],
["Pizarro conquers the Inca Empire", "Francisco Pizarro captures the Inca ruler Atahualpa and takes over an empire already weakened by civil war and disease.", [], "Spain gains the Andes and the silver mines that fund Spanish power.", ["The Inca ruler Atahualpa was captured.", "Andean silver later poured into Spain."], ["It was the conquest of Mexico.", "Portugal carried it out."]],
// ---- 2.2 Religious Wars ----
["Peace of Augsburg", "Charles V accepts that each German prince may choose Catholicism or Lutheranism for his territory.", ["Charles V"], "Calvinism is left out, a gap that helps cause the Thirty Years' War.", ["Each prince chose his territory's religion.", "Calvinism was not recognized."], ["It gave every individual freedom of religion.", "It recognized Calvinism."]],
["St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre", "During the Paris wedding of Henry of Navarre, Catholic mobs kill Admiral Coligny and thousands of Huguenots in Paris and the provinces.", ["Catherine de Médicis", "Admiral Coligny"], "Huguenots begin arguing that tyrants can be resisted, and the wars grow fiercer.", ["It happened around a royal wedding in Paris.", "Coligny was among those killed."], ["Huguenots massacred Catholics.", "It ended the Wars of Religion."]],
["Union of Utrecht", "The northern, mostly Protestant provinces of the Netherlands unite against Spain.", ["William of Orange"], "They become the Dutch Republic; the southern provinces stay Spanish and Catholic.", ["It united the northern provinces.", "The southern provinces stayed Spanish."], ["It united all 17 provinces permanently.", "It ended the Thirty Years' War."]],
["Spanish Armada defeated", "Philip II sends a huge fleet to invade England. English fire ships and storms scatter it, and it never links up with Spain's army in the Netherlands.", ["Philip II", "Elizabeth I"], "England survives as a Protestant power, and Philip II's Catholic crusade suffers a major blow.", ["Fire ships helped break up the fleet.", "Storms wrecked many ships on the way home."], ["Spanish troops landed and took London.", "It was sent to support the Huguenots."]],
["Edict of Nantes", "Henry IV keeps Catholicism as France's official faith but gives Huguenots freedom of worship in many places, civil rights, and fortified towns.", ["Henry IV"], "The wars end; Louis XIV revokes the edict in 1685.", ["Huguenots got fortified towns.", "Catholicism stayed the official religion."], ["It made Calvinism France's official religion.", "Louis XIV issued it."]],
// ---- 2.3 Thirty Years' War ----
["Defenestration of Prague", "Protestant Bohemian nobles throw two of Ferdinand's officials and a secretary out of a castle window in Prague to protest his restrictions on Protestants.", ["Ferdinand II"], "Bohemia rebels and picks Frederick V as king, starting the Thirty Years' War.", ["The officials survived the fall.", "It protested restrictions on Protestants in Bohemia."], ["The victims were Protestant ministers.", "It took place in Vienna."]],
["Battle of White Mountain", "Ferdinand's Catholic forces crush Frederick V's Bohemian army outside Prague.", ["Ferdinand II", "Frederick V"], "Bohemia is forced back to Catholicism; Frederick flees and is mocked as the 'Winter King.'", ["Frederick V lost.", "Bohemia came back under Catholic Habsburg rule."], ["Sweden won it.", "It ended the war."]],
["Edict of Restitution", "Ferdinand II orders all church lands Protestants had taken since 1552 returned to Catholics, and Calvinism stays banned.", ["Ferdinand II"], "Protestant princes are alarmed, and Sweden enters the war.", ["It covered church lands taken since 1552.", "It alarmed Protestant princes."], ["It granted Calvinists toleration.", "It gave Denmark new land in Germany."]],
["Battle of Lützen", "Gustavus Adolphus's Swedish army beats Wallenstein's imperial forces, but Gustavus is killed in the battle.", ["Gustavus Adolphus", "Wallenstein"], "Sweden wins but loses its king, and the Protestant side weakens.", ["Sweden won.", "Gustavus Adolphus died."], ["Wallenstein was killed in the battle.", "France fought on the emperor's side."]],
["France enters the war", "Catholic France, led by Cardinal Richelieu, openly declares war on Catholic Spain and fights the Habsburgs.", ["Cardinal Richelieu"], "The war becomes a political struggle against Habsburg power, and France comes out stronger.", ["France fought the Catholic Habsburgs.", "Richelieu had already funded Protestant Sweden."], ["France joined the Habsburg side.", "France entered to defend the pope's lands."]],
["Peace of Westphalia", "Treaties at Münster and Osnabrück end the war: Calvinism is recognized, German princes become nearly sovereign, and Dutch and Swiss independence is confirmed.", [], "The Holy Roman Empire stays divided into hundreds of states; France and Sweden gain land.", ["Calvinism was recognized.", "German princes became nearly sovereign."], ["The emperor gained control over the princes.", "Spain won back the Dutch provinces."]],
// ---- 2.4 England ----
["Petition of Right", "Parliament forces Charles I to accept limits: no taxes without Parliament's consent and no imprisonment without cause.", ["Charles I"], "Charles ignores it and soon rules without Parliament.", ["It banned taxes without Parliament's consent.", "Charles accepted it to get money from Parliament."], ["It created the Church of England.", "It abolished the monarchy."]],
["English Civil War begins", "Charles I raises his standard at Nottingham, and the Cavaliers (royalists) fight the Roundheads (Parliament).", ["Charles I", "Oliver Cromwell"], "Parliament wins with Cromwell's New Model Army.", ["Royalists were called Cavaliers.", "Cromwell's New Model Army won for Parliament."], ["The Roundheads fought for the king.", "It began after James II fled."]],
["Charles I executed", "After his defeat, Charles I is tried by Parliament for treason and beheaded.", ["Charles I", "Oliver Cromwell"], "England becomes a republic, later ruled by Cromwell as Lord Protector.", ["He was tried for treason.", "England became a republic."], ["He died in battle.", "His son took the throne right away."]],
["Restoration of Charles II", "After Cromwell's death, Parliament invites Charles II back. The monarchy returns, but Parliament stays powerful.", ["Charles II"], "Stuart rule resumes until James II's Catholic policies cause a new crisis.", ["Parliament invited him back.", "It followed Cromwell's death."], ["It made Charles II an absolute monarch.", "Charles II legally made England Catholic."]],
["Glorious Revolution", "Parliament invites William of Orange and Mary to take the throne from the Catholic James II, who flees to France.", ["William III", "James II"], "The 1689 Bill of Rights limits the crown: constitutional monarchy.", ["James II fled to France.", "Parliament invited William and Mary."], ["James II was executed.", "It made Cromwell king."]],
["English Bill of Rights", "Parliament spells out the crown's limits: no suspending laws, no taxes or peacetime army without Parliament's consent, and free elections.", ["William III"], "Parliament's supremacy is settled.", ["No taxes without Parliament's consent.", "No peacetime standing army without Parliament's consent."], ["It gave Catholics full religious equality.", "It made the king the head of Parliament."]],
// ---- 2.5 Louis XIV ----
["The Fronde begins", "Nobles and the Parlement of Paris revolt against Mazarin's centralizing, tax-raising government, forcing the boy king Louis XIV to flee Paris.", ["Cardinal Mazarin", "Louis XIV"], "The revolt fails, and Louis learns to distrust powerful nobles and Paris.", ["Young Louis XIV had to flee Paris.", "Nobles and the Parlement of Paris rebelled."], ["It was a Huguenot uprising.", "It permanently limited royal power."]],
["Louis XIV begins personal rule", "When Mazarin dies, Louis XIV announces he will govern without a chief minister.", ["Louis XIV", "Cardinal Mazarin"], "Louis centralizes power, uses Versailles to tame the nobles, and relies on ministers like Colbert.", ["He refused to appoint a chief minister.", "It followed Mazarin's death."], ["It followed a successful Fronde.", "He shared power with the Estates General."]],
["Edict of Nantes revoked", "Louis XIV revokes the edict, closing Protestant churches and schools and pressuring Huguenots to convert.", ["Louis XIV"], "Around a quarter million Huguenots flee, many of them skilled workers, to Protestant lands.", ["Huguenots fled abroad.", "Protestant churches were closed."], ["It gave Huguenots more rights.", "Henry IV did it."]],
["War of the Spanish Succession begins", "Charles II of Spain leaves his empire to Louis XIV's grandson. A Grand Alliance goes to war to stop France and Spain from uniting.", ["Philip V", "Louis XIV", "William III"], "The war ends with the Treaty of Utrecht (1713).", ["A Grand Alliance fought to keep France and Spain apart.", "It began over Louis XIV's grandson inheriting Spain."], ["It was fought to restore the Stuarts.", "Spain and Britain fought together against France."]],
["Treaty of Utrecht", "Philip V keeps Spain, but France and Spain may never unite. Britain gains Gibraltar and Minorca, and Austria gets Spain's Netherlands and Italian lands.", ["Philip V"], "A balance of power returns, and Britain begins its rise.", ["Britain gained Gibraltar.", "France and Spain could never unite."], ["Philip V lost the Spanish throne.", "France gained the Spanish Netherlands."]],
// ---- 2.6 Austria, Prussia, Russia ----
["Siege of Vienna", "An Ottoman army besieges the Habsburg capital until a relief army led by Polish king John III Sobieski breaks the siege.", ["John III Sobieski"], "The Habsburgs then push the Ottomans out of Hungary.", ["A Polish king led the relief.", "The Habsburgs then won back Hungary."], ["The Ottomans captured Vienna.", "Peter the Great broke the siege."]],
["St. Petersburg founded", "Peter the Great builds a new capital on the Baltic coast, on land taken from Sweden, as Russia's window on the West.", ["Peter the Great"], "Russia's government and elite turn west, and the capital moves from Moscow.", ["It was built on land taken from Sweden.", "It faces the Baltic Sea."], ["It was built on the Black Sea.", "Catherine the Great founded it."]],
["Battle of Poltava", "Peter the Great's rebuilt army crushes Charles XII of Sweden in Ukraine.", ["Peter the Great", "Charles XII"], "Sweden's era as a great power ends; the Peace of Nystad (1721) gives Russia the Baltic coast.", ["Russia defeated Sweden.", "It was fought in Ukraine."], ["Sweden went on to capture Moscow.", "Poland won it."]],
["Pragmatic Sanction", "Emperor Charles VI, who has no son, issues a decree so his daughter Maria Theresa can inherit all the Habsburg lands, and spends years getting other powers to accept it.", ["Charles VI", "Maria Theresa"], "When he dies in 1740, Frederick II ignores it and invades Silesia.", ["It let a daughter inherit the Habsburg lands.", "Prussia ignored it in 1740."], ["It made Austria a republic.", "Peter the Great issued it."]],
["Table of Ranks", "Peter creates 14 ranks in the army, navy, and civil service. Nobles rise through service, and commoners who reach a high enough rank become nobles.", ["Peter the Great"], "The nobility is bound to state service.", ["It had 14 ranks.", "Commoners could earn noble status through service."], ["It ranked Russia's Orthodox bishops.", "It abolished the nobility."]],
// ---- 3.1 France after Louis XIV ----
["Duke of Orléans becomes regent", "With five-year-old Louis XV on the throne, the Duke of Orléans governs and restores the parlements' right to object to royal decrees.", ["Duke of Orléans"], "The parlements regain power to block royal reform, a problem all century.", ["He restored the parlements' power.", "Louis XV was a small child."], ["He was Louis XV's father.", "He abolished the parlements."]],
["Mississippi and South Sea Bubbles burst", "John Law's bank issues paper money and sells shares in the Mississippi Company. Speculation soars, then collapses when people try to cash out; Britain's South Sea Bubble bursts the same year.", ["John Law"], "Investors are ruined, and France distrusts paper money and banks for decades.", ["John Law's bank issued paper money.", "Britain had a similar bubble the same year."], ["It paid off France's debt for a generation.", "Louis XIV personally directed it."]],
["Cardinal Fleury takes charge", "Louis XV's old tutor, Cardinal Fleury, runs the government, avoiding war and steadying finances.", ["Cardinal Fleury"], "France has relative peace and prosperity until the 1740s.", ["He had been Louis XV's tutor.", "He kept France mostly at peace."], ["He started the Seven Years' War.", "He founded the Jesuits."]],
["Bonnie Prince Charlie's rising", "Charles Edward Stuart lands in Scotland to win back the throne for the Catholic Stuarts and marches into England, but turns back and is crushed at Culloden in 1746.", [], "The Jacobite cause collapses, and Britain cracks down on the Highlands.", ["It was crushed at Culloden.", "It aimed to restore the Catholic Stuarts."], ["It captured London.", "Prussia backed it."]],
["Viceroyalty of Río de la Plata created", "Charles III reorganizes Spanish South America, creating a new viceroyalty centered on Buenos Aires.", ["Charles III"], "Part of the Bourbon reforms that tighten royal control over the colonies.", ["It was centered on Buenos Aires.", "It was part of the Bourbon reforms."], ["It created an independent republic.", "Portugal created it."]],
// ---- 3.2 Atlantic slavery and global wars ----
["Frederick II invades Silesia", "Frederick II seizes the rich Habsburg province of Silesia, ignoring the Pragmatic Sanction and starting the War of the Austrian Succession.", ["Frederick II (the Great)", "Maria Theresa"], "Prussia keeps Silesia at Aix-la-Chapelle (1748), starting a long rivalry with Austria.", ["He ignored the Pragmatic Sanction.", "Silesia was a rich Habsburg province."], ["He invaded Poland.", "Austria took Silesia back right away."]],
["Diplomatic Revolution", "Britain allies with Prussia, so France switches to ally with its old enemy Austria.", ["Maria Theresa", "Frederick II (the Great)"], "The alliances for the Seven Years' War are set.", ["France allied with Austria.", "Britain allied with Prussia."], ["Prussia allied with Austria.", "Britain allied with France."]],
["Seven Years' War begins", "Frederick II invades Saxony, and the war spreads to North America and India.", ["Frederick II (the Great)", "William Pitt the Elder"], "A truly global war between Britain and France.", ["Frederick began by invading Saxony.", "It was fought in Europe, North America, and India."], ["Frederick began by invading Silesia again.", "It was fought only in Europe."]],
["Battle of Plassey", "Robert Clive's British East India Company forces defeat the ruler of Bengal and his French allies.", ["Robert Clive"], "Britain dominates Bengal and becomes the leading European power in India.", ["Robert Clive led the British.", "It gave Britain power in Bengal."], ["France won control of India.", "It was fought in Canada."]],
["Treaty of Paris (1763)", "France gives Britain Canada and its lands east of the Mississippi, and Britain keeps its dominance in India.", ["William Pitt the Elder"], "Britain is dominant but deep in debt, which leads to taxes on its colonies.", ["Britain gained Canada.", "It left Britain deep in debt."], ["France gained Canada.", "It recognized American independence."]],
// ---- 3.3 Scientific Revolution ----
["Copernicus' On the Revolutions", "Copernicus argues the sun, not Earth, is at the center of the universe, though he keeps circular orbits and epicycles.", ["Nicolaus Copernicus"], "Heliocentrism challenges Ptolemy and the Church's picture of the universe.", ["He kept circular orbits.", "It was published in 1543."], ["He discovered elliptical orbits.", "He used a telescope."]],
["Kepler's New Astronomy", "Using Tycho Brahe's precise observations, Kepler shows that planets move in ellipses, not circles.", ["Johannes Kepler", "Tycho Brahe"], "The sun-centered system finally matches observations.", ["He used Brahe's data.", "Planets move in ellipses."], ["He proved Earth is the center.", "He discovered universal gravitation."]],
["Galileo condemned", "After his Dialogue defends Copernicus, the Roman Inquisition forces Galileo to recant and places him under house arrest.", ["Galileo Galilei"], "A lasting symbol of conflict between the Church and the new science.", ["He was forced to recant.", "He lived under house arrest."], ["He was burned at the stake.", "It happened in Protestant England."]],
["Bacon's Novum Organum", "Francis Bacon argues knowledge should come from observation and experiment, reasoning inductively from evidence.", ["Francis Bacon"], "Empiricism becomes a pillar of the scientific method.", ["It promoted induction.", "It attacked reliance on ancient authority."], ["It argued for doubting everything and reasoning from first principles.", "It proved heliocentrism with a telescope."]],
["Descartes' Discourse on Method", "Descartes doubts everything that can be doubted and rebuilds knowledge by deduction from certain first principles: 'I think, therefore I am.'", ["René Descartes"], "Deduction becomes the other pillar of the scientific method.", ["It used deduction.", "It began by doubting everything."], ["It argued knowledge comes only from experiment.", "Bacon wrote it."]],
["Newton's Principia", "Newton shows that universal gravitation and three laws of motion explain both the planets' orbits and motion on Earth.", ["Isaac Newton"], "A mechanical, law-governed universe that inspires the Enlightenment.", ["It described universal gravitation.", "It linked motion on Earth and in the heavens."], ["It defended an Earth-centered universe.", "The Council of Trent condemned it."]],
["Locke's Two Treatises of Government", "Locke argues people have natural rights to life, liberty, and property and form governments by consent to protect them.", ["John Locke"], "If government violates those rights, people may resist, an idea behind later revolutions.", ["Government rests on the consent of the governed.", "People may resist a government that violates their rights."], ["People must obey an all-powerful sovereign no matter what.", "Kings rule by divine right."]],
// ---- 3.4 Enlightenment begins ----
["Voltaire's Letters on the English", "Voltaire praises England's toleration, Parliament, and Newtonian science, an indirect criticism of France.", ["Voltaire"], "French authorities condemn it, but it spreads Newton and Locke in France.", ["It praised English toleration.", "It was an indirect attack on France."], ["It praised French absolutism.", "It attacked Newton's physics."]],
["Jean Calas executed", "A Protestant merchant in Toulouse is falsely accused of killing his son to stop him from converting to Catholicism, then tortured and executed.", ["Voltaire"], "Voltaire's campaign clears Calas's name and makes the case a symbol of fanaticism.", ["Calas was a Protestant.", "Voltaire campaigned to clear his name."], ["Calas was a Catholic priest.", "He was acquitted at trial."]],
["Voltaire's Candide", "A satirical novel in which a naive hero suffers disaster after disaster while his tutor insists this is the best of all possible worlds.", ["Voltaire"], "It mocks blind optimism and attacks religious and political abuses.", ["It mocks blind optimism.", "Voltaire wrote it."], ["It praises the idea that this is the best of all possible worlds.", "It is a treatise on separation of powers."]],
["Kant's 'What Is Enlightenment?'", "Kant defines Enlightenment as escaping self-imposed immaturity: dare to know, and use your own reason.", ["Immanuel Kant"], "A classic statement of the Enlightenment's goal.", ["Its motto is 'Dare to know.'", "Kant wrote in Königsberg, Prussia."], ["Kant said people should rely on authority.", "Voltaire wrote it."]],
["Lessing's Nathan the Wise", "Lessing's play features a wise Jewish merchant and argues for toleration among Jews, Christians, and Muslims.", ["Gotthold Lessing"], "A landmark plea for religious toleration.", ["It argued for toleration among three religions.", "Its hero is a Jewish merchant."], ["It argued Christianity is the only true faith.", "Moses Mendelssohn wrote it."]],
// ---- 3.5 Enlightenment applied ----
["Montesquieu's Spirit of the Laws", "Montesquieu argues that executive, legislative, and judicial powers should be separated and check each other, with nobles and parlements limiting the monarch.", ["Montesquieu"], "Separation of powers later shapes the U.S. Constitution.", ["It called for separation of powers.", "It valued intermediary bodies like the parlements."], ["It called for direct democracy.", "It argued for absolute monarchy."]],
["First volume of the Encyclopedia", "Diderot and d'Alembert launch a huge reference work collecting practical and scientific knowledge and quietly criticizing church and state.", ["Denis Diderot"], "Finished in 1772 at 28 volumes, it spreads Enlightenment ideas widely.", ["Diderot and d'Alembert edited it.", "It treated knowledge in secular terms."], ["The Jesuits published it.", "It was a single short pamphlet."]],
["Rousseau's Social Contract", "Rousseau argues the community's general will should rule, and that true freedom means obeying laws made by the people as a whole.", ["Jean-Jacques Rousseau"], "Its ideas inspire radical democracy and Robespierre's Jacobins.", ["It centers on the general will.", "It influenced the Jacobins."], ["It argued for separation of powers.", "It praised free-market self-interest."]],
["Beccaria's On Crimes and Punishments", "Beccaria attacks torture and the death penalty, arguing that punishment should prevent crime and serve the greatest good for the greatest number.", ["Cesare Beccaria"], "It inspires legal reforms across Europe.", ["It opposed torture.", "It used utilitarian reasoning."], ["It called for harsher punishments.", "Adam Smith wrote it."]],
["Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations", "Smith argues that individuals pursuing self-interest in free markets create national wealth, and he attacks mercantilism.", ["Adam Smith"], "The founding text of laissez-faire economics.", ["It attacked mercantilism.", "It praised free markets and the division of labor."], ["It said wealth is gold hoarded by the state.", "It called for government price controls."]],
["Mendelssohn's Jerusalem", "Moses Mendelssohn argues for a religiously neutral state and civil rights for Jews, and for reason within Judaism.", ["Moses Mendelssohn"], "A key text of the Jewish Enlightenment (Haskalah).", ["It called for a religiously neutral state.", "Its author led the Haskalah."], ["It called for a Jewish state in Palestine.", "It rejected reason in religion."]],
// ---- 3.6 American Revolution ----
["Stamp Act", "Parliament taxes colonial newspapers, legal documents, and other printed items to help pay for defending the empire.", [], "Colonists boycott British goods and protest taxation without representation; it is repealed in 1766.", ["It taxed printed materials.", "Colonists answered with boycotts."], ["It taxed tea.", "Colonists paid it without protest."]],
["Stamp Act repealed; Declaratory Act", "Parliament repeals the Stamp Act after boycotts hurt British merchants, but declares it can legislate for the colonies in all cases.", [], "The constitutional dispute stays unresolved.", ["The Stamp Act was repealed.", "Parliament claimed full authority over the colonies."], ["Parliament gave up its right to tax the colonies.", "It granted independence."]],
["Boston Tea Party", "Colonists dump East India Company tea into Boston Harbor to protest a tax they never consented to.", ["Lord North"], "Parliament responds with the Intolerable Acts.", ["The tea was taxed.", "Parliament answered with the Intolerable Acts."], ["It protested the Stamp Act.", "British troops dumped the tea."]],
["Paine's Common Sense", "Thomas Paine argues that monarchy is absurd and that it is common sense for America to declare independence.", ["Thomas Paine"], "It swings public opinion toward independence months before July 1776.", ["It argued for independence.", "It attacked monarchy itself."], ["It argued for reconciliation with Britain.", "Edmund Burke wrote it."]],
["France allies with the Americans", "After the American victory at Saratoga, France signs an alliance with the United States, helped by Benjamin Franklin's diplomacy.", [], "French money, troops, and ships help win at Yorktown (1781) but deepen France's debt.", ["Franklin helped win French support.", "It deepened France's debt."], ["France sided with Britain.", "It came after Yorktown."]],
["Treaty of Paris (1783)", "Britain recognizes American independence.", [], "Britain loses its American colonies but keeps Canada; France's war debts head toward crisis.", ["Britain recognized American independence.", "France came out of the war deeper in debt."], ["Britain gained Louisiana.", "It ended the Seven Years' War."]],
// ---- 4.1 Road to 1789 ----
["Calonne's reform plan", "Finance minister Calonne proposes a land tax every landowner would pay regardless of status, along with freer internal trade and lower salt taxes.", ["Charles Alexandre de Calonne"], "Nobles and clergy see it as an attack on their privileges.", ["The land tax would apply regardless of status.", "It threatened noble and church privileges."], ["It proposed taxing only peasants.", "Necker proposed it."]],
["Assembly of Notables meets", "Hand-picked nobles and clergy reject Calonne's plan, ask for Necker back, and say only the Estates General can approve new taxes.", ["Charles Alexandre de Calonne"], "Calonne falls, and the road leads to calling the Estates General.", ["The Notables rejected the plan.", "They said only the Estates General could approve taxes."], ["They approved the land tax.", "The Third Estate elected its members."]],
["Doubling the Third", "The royal council gives the Third Estate twice as many deputies as each other estate, but leaves the voting method undecided.", ["Jacques Necker"], "If voting is by head, the Third Estate, with liberal nobles and clergy, can win.", ["The voting method was left undecided.", "It doubled the Third Estate's deputies."], ["It guaranteed voting by head.", "It doubled the nobles' deputies."]],
["National Assembly declared", "After weeks of deadlock over voting, the Third Estate declares itself the National Assembly, representing the nation.", ["Abbé Sieyès"], "Some clergy and nobles join; the king locks them out, leading to the Tennis Court Oath.", ["It followed a deadlock over voting.", "Sieyès helped lead it."], ["The king created it.", "Only nobles joined it."]],
["Tennis Court Oath", "Locked out of their meeting hall, the deputies gather at an indoor tennis court and swear not to separate until France has a constitution.", [], "Louis XVI gives in on June 27 and orders the other estates to join.", ["They met at an indoor tennis court.", "They vowed to write a constitution."], ["They swore loyalty to the king's absolute power.", "It took place at the Bastille."]],
// ---- 4.2 Revolution of 1789 ----
["Fall of the Bastille", "After Louis fires Necker and gathers troops, Parisian shopkeepers, artisans, and workers storm the Bastille fortress for gunpowder and weapons.", [], "The king backs down; the Bastille becomes a symbol of the people defeating royal power.", ["Crowds wanted gunpowder and weapons.", "Only seven prisoners were inside, none political."], ["Hundreds of political prisoners were freed.", "The National Assembly ordered it."]],
["The Great Fear", "Rumors spread through the countryside that troops or brigands would seize peasants' crops. Peasants attack châteaux and burn the records of what they owe their lords.", [], "The Assembly abolishes privileges on August 4 to calm the countryside.", ["Peasants burned feudal records.", "Rumors drove it."], ["It happened mainly in Paris.", "The clergy led it."]],
["Night of August 4", "Nobles and clergy in the Assembly give up feudal dues, hunting rights, tithes, and tax exemptions in a single night.", [], "The old order of privilege legally ends.", ["Tithes were abolished.", "Nobles gave up their tax exemptions."], ["It restored feudal dues.", "The pope forced it."]],
["Declaration of the Rights of Man and Citizen", "The Assembly declares natural rights to liberty, property, and security, equality before the law, and that sovereignty rests with the nation.", ["Olympe de Gouges"], "Women are left out, prompting Olympe de Gouges's Declaration of the Rights of Woman.", ["It said sovereignty rests with the nation.", "It did not extend rights to women."], ["It gave women the vote.", "It kept noble privileges."]],
["Women's March on Versailles", "Thousands of Parisian women march to Versailles demanding bread, force the king to accept the Assembly's decrees, and bring the royal family back to Paris.", ["Marquis de Lafayette"], "The king and Assembly now sit in Paris, under pressure from the crowds.", ["They demanded bread.", "The royal family moved to Paris."], ["They were demanding the right to vote.", "The king fled to Austria afterward."]],
["Civil Constitution of the Clergy", "The Assembly makes the Church a branch of the state: clergy are elected and paid by the state and must swear an oath of loyalty.", ["Pius VI"], "Many priests refuse the oath and the pope condemns it, splitting France.", ["Clergy were elected and paid by the state.", "Many priests refused the oath."], ["It gave the Church back its lands.", "The pope approved it."]],
["Flight to Varennes", "Louis XVI and his family flee Paris in disguise toward the border but are recognized and caught at Varennes.", ["Louis XVI", "Marie Antoinette"], "Trust in the king collapses, and Austria and Prussia answer with the Declaration of Pillnitz.", ["The royal family was caught at Varennes.", "They traveled in disguise."], ["They reached Austria safely.", "It happened after the king's trial."]],
// ---- 4.3 Second revolution and war ----
["France declares war on Austria", "Pushed by the Girondists, the Legislative Assembly declares war on Austria; the king hopes a French defeat will restore his power.", ["Louis XVI"], "Early defeats radicalize Paris.", ["The Girondists pushed for war.", "The king hoped France would lose."], ["The Mountain forced it through over Girondist objections.", "Austria had already invaded Paris."]],
["Brunswick Manifesto", "The Prussian commander, the Duke of Brunswick, threatens to destroy Paris if the royal family is harmed.", [], "It backfires and fuels the attack on the Tuileries on August 10.", ["It threatened Paris.", "It backfired."], ["Louis XVI issued it.", "It calmed Paris."]],
["Monarchy overthrown", "Crowds and National Guards storm the Tuileries Palace; the royal family flees to the Assembly and is imprisoned, and the monarchy is suspended.", ["Louis XVI"], "A new Convention elected by all adult men declares France a republic in September.", ["The Tuileries Palace was stormed.", "It led to a Convention elected by all men."], ["The king was executed that day.", "It happened at Versailles."]],
["September Massacres", "Fearing traitors in the prisons as Prussian troops advanced, Paris crowds kill about 1,200 prisoners, mostly ordinary criminals.", [], "A sign of the violence to come, and it hardens foreign opinion against France.", ["About 1,200 prisoners were killed.", "Most victims were ordinary criminals."], ["Royalists carried it out.", "It happened in the Vendée."]],
["Louis XVI executed", "The Convention tries Louis XVI for treason, convicts him by a large majority, and sends him to the guillotine.", ["Louis XVI"], "Europe's monarchs are horrified, and the war widens.", ["The Convention tried him.", "He was guillotined."], ["He died during the Flight to Varennes.", "He was acquitted."]],
["Committee of Public Safety created", "The Convention creates a small executive committee with near-dictatorial power to run the war and crush internal enemies.", ["Maximilien Robespierre"], "Under Robespierre it runs the Reign of Terror.", ["It had near-dictatorial power.", "Robespierre became its leading figure."], ["The sans-culottes elected it.", "It was created to protect the king."]],
["Levée en masse", "The republic drafts young men and puts the whole nation to work for the war: married men make weapons, women make tents and clothes, children make bandages.", ["Lazare Carnot"], "France fields huge armies of 800,000 or more: the first total war.", ["It mobilized the whole nation.", "Carnot organized it."], ["It hired foreign mercenaries.", "It drafted only nobles."]],
// ---- 4.4 Terror, Thermidor, Directory ----
["Women's clubs banned", "After clashes involving the Society of Revolutionary Republican Women, the Jacobins ban all women's political clubs, citing Rousseau's 'separate spheres.'", ["Pauline Léon"], "Women are pushed out of formal politics.", ["The Jacobins banned them.", "They cited separate spheres for men and women."], ["Women won the right to vote.", "Royalists banned them."]],
["Notre Dame becomes a Temple of Reason", "During de-Christianization, the cathedral becomes a Temple of Reason, and a revolutionary calendar replaces the Christian one.", [], "Robespierre later replaces the Cult of Reason with the Cult of the Supreme Being.", ["A revolutionary calendar replaced the Christian one.", "It was part of de-Christianization."], ["The pope approved it.", "It happened under Napoleon."]],
["Law of 22 Prairial", "Revolutionary tribunals can now convict without real evidence or defense lawyers, and the only verdicts are acquittal or death.", ["Maximilien Robespierre"], "Executions in Paris surge in the summer of 1794.", ["Defendants lost the right to a lawyer.", "Executions surged."], ["It ended the Terror.", "It created the Directory."]],
["9 Thermidor: Robespierre falls", "Fearing they are next, Convention members shout Robespierre down; he is arrested and guillotined the next day.", ["Maximilien Robespierre"], "The Terror ends, and the Thermidorian Reaction begins.", ["He was guillotined the next day.", "His fellow deputies turned on him."], ["The sans-culottes rescued him.", "Napoleon arrested him."]],
["Constitution of the Year III", "A new constitution creates a two-house legislature and a five-man Directory, with property requirements for voting.", [], "A republic of property owners that rejects both monarchy and democracy.", ["It created a five-man Directory.", "It had property requirements for voting."], ["It created direct democracy.", "It restored the monarchy."]],
["13 Vendémiaire", "When royalists rise in Paris, Napoleon breaks the uprising with artillery: the 'whiff of grapeshot.'", ["Napoleon Bonaparte"], "The uprising fails, and Napoleon becomes a rising star.", ["Napoleon used artillery.", "The rebels were royalists."], ["The rebels were sans-culottes demanding bread.", "Napoleon joined the rebels."]],
["18 Brumaire: Napoleon's coup", "With Sieyès and others, Napoleon overthrows the Directory and takes power.", ["Napoleon Bonaparte", "Abbé Sieyès"], "The revolution ends in rule by a general.", ["Sieyès was one of the plotters.", "It overthrew the Directory."], ["It restored the Bourbon monarchy.", "It happened in 1789."]]
];
